'''Sant Cristòfor International Airport''' ({{lang|ca|Aeroport Internacional de Sant Cristòfor}}; [[IATA airport code|IATA]]: SCI, [[ICAO airport code|ICAO]]: SCIB) is the busiest airport in [[Balboa]] and the principal international gateway to the [[Isthmus of Balboa]]. Located in the [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|ciutat of Sant Martí]] in eastern [[Sant Cristòfor]], the airport serves the metropolitan area of approximately 5.16 million people and the nearby [[International City of Areza]]. The airport is a hub for the flag carrier [[Aeroístmus]] and the low-cost carrier [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]].

The airport is notable for its dual-code arrangement: the '''Areza Concourse''' of Terminal C, serving flights where all passengers are ticketed to the [[International City of Areza]], operates under the secondary IATA code '''ARZ''' (ICAO: SCAR). This arrangement, unique in Adonia, was established as part of the [[Adonian Community]]'s consolidation of air services to Areza following the closure of [[Areza International Airport]] in 2003.

== History ==

=== Planning and construction ===

The need for a new airport to serve Sant Cristòfor was identified in the early 1970s, as the existing airport at [[Ciutat de l'Estació (ciutat)|Ciutat de l'Estació]] — which had been rebuilt in 1954 during the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|AC mandate period]] — was constrained by its urban location and could not be expanded to accommodate jet-era traffic. The [[Balboa Airports Authority]] was established in 1975 to oversee the planning, construction, and operation of the new facility.

A site in [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|Sant Martí]] was selected in 1977, on flat agricultural land approximately 12 km east of the city centre. The site offered room for expansion, clear approaches over the coastal plain, and proximity to the planned Metro Line 1 extension. Construction began in 1979 and was completed in 1984; the airport opened on 15 March 1985.

=== Two-terminal era (1985–2008) ===

[[File:Sant Cristofor Airport Terminal A and B 1985 placeholder.jpg|thumb|250px|Terminals A and B at opening in 1985]]

The airport opened with two terminals: Terminal A (domestic) and Terminal B (international, serving Abyalan and Lurandian routes). The two terminals were connected by a central atrium with shared check-in, security, and baggage claim facilities. The initial capacity was approximately 12 million passengers per year.

[[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 1]] was extended to the airport in 1986, providing a direct rail link to the city centre via the [[Grand Train Terminal]]. The airport rapidly exceeded its design capacity, handling 18 million passengers by 1995, and congestion in Terminal B became a persistent issue.

The closure of [[Areza International Airport]] — the former [[Ciutat de l'Estació]] airport that had served the [[International City of Areza]] since 1954 — was mandated by the [[Adonian Community]] as part of the consolidation of Areza's transport infrastructure. The closure was completed in 2003, coinciding with the opening of Terminal C and [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] — running from Sant Martí through the canal corridor to Areza. The Line 9 extension was built with bypass tracks at all stations, allowing express services to skip intermediate stops and provide a direct, non-stop connection between the airport and Areza in approximately 45 minutes.

=== Terminal C and modern expansion (2003–present) ===

[[File:Sant Cristofor Airport Terminal C opening 2003 placeholder.jpg|thumb|250px|Terminal C at its opening in 2003, with the Areza Concourse wing visible to the right]]

Terminal C opened in 2003, built to relieve chronic congestion in Terminal B and to accommodate the growth of long-haul international traffic driven by Sant Cristòfor's expanding role as a financial hub. The [[Adonian Community]] funded the construction of the '''Areza Concourse''' — a dedicated wing of Terminal C with its own landside check-in, customs, and immigration facilities for ARZ-coded flights. Passengers on ARZ-coded flights clear [[International City of Areza|Areza]] customs and immigration within the Areza Concourse before proceeding through security into the shared airside departure concourse, where they mingle with other Terminal C international passengers. The Areza Concourse operates under the secondary IATA code '''ARZ''' (ICAO: SCAR), making Sant Cristòfor one of the few airports in Adonia with dual IATA codes. Terminal C is the largest terminal at the airport and is connected to the main complex by an extension of the central atrium.

A third runway (06/24) was added in 2008, built to increase passenger capacity and to separate cargo operations from passenger traffic.

== Infrastructure ==

=== Terminals ===

[[File:Sant Cristofor Airport terminal layout placeholder.jpg|thumb|250px|Terminal layout of Sant Cristòfor International Airport]]

The airport has three terminals:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Terminal !! Opened !! Function !! Gates !! Annual capacity
|-
| A || 1985 || Domestic || 18 || ~10 million
|-
| B || 1985 || Short-haul international (Abyala / Lurandia) || 22 || ~12 million
|-
| C || 2003 || Long-haul international (Kaftia / Illypnia / Fosia) + Areza Concourse (ARZ code) || 28 || ~15 million
|-
| '''Total''' || || '''68''' || '''~37 million'''
|}

Terminals A, B, and C are connected by a central atrium with shared check-in, security screening, and baggage claim facilities on the landside, and post-security airside corridors linking the gate areas. Terminal B is used for short-haul international routes operated with smaller aircraft, while Terminal C serves long-haul and premium international routes.

The '''Areza Concourse''' is a dedicated wing of Terminal C with its own landside check-in, security, customs, and baggage claim facilities. [[File:Sant Cristofor Airport Areza Concourse placeholder.jpg|thumb|250px|The Areza Concourse landside check-in area, with Areza customs and immigration facilities]], security, customs, and baggage claim facilities. Passengers on ARZ-coded flights clear [[International City of Areza|Areza]] customs and immigration within the Areza Concourse rather than Balboan, and do not enter Balboan territory. After clearing Areza customs and security, passengers proceed into the shared Terminal C airside departure concourse. This distinction is significant because Areza maintains a 15-day open visa policy for all [[Adonian Community]] member states, whereas Balboa applies its own separate visa requirements. Passengers travelling to Areza who would require a Balboan visa for transit through the main terminals can therefore use ARZ-coded flights without needing to meet Balboan immigration requirements.

The dual-code arrangement shapes airline routing decisions: countries with favourable visa relationships with Balboa generally route their passengers through the main SCI terminals, while those whose nationals would face Balboan visa restrictions route through the ARZ-coded Areza Concourse to access Areza's more open visa regime.

A '''transfer desk''' in Terminal C allows passengers arriving on SCI-coded flights who wish to travel to Areza to transfer to the Areza Concourse landside, where they clear Areza customs and immigration rather than Balboan. This enables passengers on any international flight to reach Areza without passing through Balboan border control, even if their flight was not ARZ-coded.

The Areza Concourse has no public arrivals or departures area for private vehicles. Access to the Areza Concourse is restricted to authorised transport only: the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] express service, scheduled bus services, chartered car services, and [[Adonian Community]] government vehicles. Private vehicles are not permitted at the Areza Concourse. Authorised ground vehicles travelling between the Areza Concourse and Areza are restricted to the [[E54 expressway]], a dedicated road that runs directly between the airport and Areza without intermediate junctions or access to the Balboan road network; vehicles using the E54 are not permitted to stop in Balboa and may only travel between the airport and Areza.

=== Runways ===

[[File:Sant Cristofor Airport runways aerial placeholder.jpg|thumb|250px|Aerial view of the three runways, with Terminal C visible at centre]]

The airport has three runways:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Direction !! Length !! Surface !! Use
|-
| 09/27 || 3,200 m || Asphalt || Primary; all commercial passenger operations
|-
| 15/33 || 2,600 m || Asphalt || Secondary; domestic, general aviation, relief
|-
| 06/24 || 2,800 m || Asphalt || Cargo; also passenger overflow during peak
|}

Runway 09/27 is the primary runway, handling the majority of commercial passenger operations. Runway 15/33 is used for domestic services, general aviation, and as a relief runway during peak periods. Runway 06/24, opened in 2008, was built primarily to separate cargo operations from passenger traffic, but also serves as passenger overflow during peak periods. All three runways are equipped with [[Instrument Landing System|ILS]] Category II approach systems.

=== Cargo facilities ===

[[File:Sant Cristofor Airport cargo facilities placeholder.jpg|thumb|250px|Cargo facilities on the eastern side of the airport, adjacent to Runway 15/33]]

Sant Cristòfor International Airport is a major cargo hub, reflecting the city's position at the junction of the Illypnian, Fosian, and Kaftian markets and its proximity to the [[Balboa Canal]]. The airport handled 612,000 tonnes of cargo in 2025, making it the busiest cargo airport in the isthmus region. Cargo facilities are located on the eastern side of the airport, adjacent to Runway 15/33, with dedicated cargo aprons, warehousing, and logistics facilities operated by the [[Balboa Airports Authority]] and private logistics companies.

